<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Óengus I of the Picts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 761</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Óengus son of Fergus, Óengus mac Fergusa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Óengus son of Fergus was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> from 729 to 761 and the most powerful ruler in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> of his time. Emerging from civil wars in Pictland, he defeated his rivals, campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> he took in 736, and fought Strathclyde. The Pictish over-kingship based in Fortriu reached its height under him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oengus-i-of-the-picts.jpg" alt="Óengus I of the Picts" width="310" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Son of Groucho of Scotland on Flikr. · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%93engus_detail_sarcophagus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 730s and 740s the Pictish king <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b> attacked Dál Riata, capturing Dunadd in 736.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings fought one another in recurring civil wars, and <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b> rose to power through a series of battles in 728–729 before attacking Dál Riata, whose stronghold of Dunadd he took in 736.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Selbach's defeat at sea in 719 and Pictish conquest under <b>Oengus son of Fergus</b> in the 730s broke Cenel Loairn's power.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li><li><blockquote>Atholl, Athfotla, appears in Irish annals from the 8th century, when a king of Atholl was drowned by <b>Oengus son of Fergus</b>, king of the Picts, in 739.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-atholl">Kingdom of Atholl</a></li><li><blockquote>Bridei son of Beli destroyed the Northumbrian army at Dun Nechtain in 685, ending English dominance in the north. <b>Óengus son of Fergus</b>, from 729 to 761, defeated rivals and even campaigned against Dál Riata and Strathclyde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Kingdom of Fortriu</a></li></ul></section>
